annot(#noCore)

#noCore
# An arrow whose source is a declared data type.
  A bare name is a term in its own right, so an arrow may be written from it — `/Sample= Outer(..)`
  in `recordField.ct` is one, `f= f(b= 2, c= 3)` in `defaultArrow.ct` is another. In both, the name
  on the left has no data definition of its own, and nothing in the library or the tests had ever
  put one there: `data(A)` above `A= B` is a shape this file writes first. It is the shape that says
  a rewrite rule is a rewrite rule whoever wrote its left side. A function-typed value set is as
  much a data object as a declared one, and a declared data object is just as rewritable — arrows
  between data objects are what this file is made of. Nothing may read "this name has a data
  definition" as "this name is finished"; what may happen to a value is decided by the arrows that
  exist and by the destination its consumer permits, and by nothing else.
  Three types and two arrows, so the chain is walked more than once: `B` reaching `C` is the single
  arrow found at the source, while `A` reaching `C` must go on from what that arrow produced. The
  hops are written as separate definitions so a failure says which one broke, and `isC`'s arm
  matches the VALUE `C` rather than the type, so a hop that arrives unrewritten finds no arm rather
  than quietly passing.
  Written without the core library, so that when a compiler change breaks it there are eleven lines
  to read rather than a standard library.
data(A)
data(B)
data(C)
data(Yes)

A= B

B= C

isC(c -> C) -> Yes
isC(c= C)= Yes

checkC= isC(c= C)

checkB= isC(c= B)

checkA= isC(c= A)
